---
title: "Dataset provenance"
description: "Citing the data a document was computed from, so a reader can re-run it."
---

A figure computed from data is only reproducible if a reader can get the same data.
Taliesin does not ship the data: a multi-gigabyte file has no business in a folder of
HTML. It ships the **provenance** instead, so the file is cited the way a source is.

## A small file, in the tree

`{{< dataset data/measurements.csv >}}` states what the file is and links it. Because
the card carries an `href`, the build copies the file into the output beside the page,
so this one really does travel with the document:

{{< dataset data/measurements.csv >}}

The size and checksum are read off the file itself, so that bare invocation already
renders a complete card. Everything you can add is something the file cannot say about
itself, and it rides the invocation rather than a separate block:

{{< dataset data/measurements.csv title="Tarn temperature profiles" licence=CC0-1.0 source=https://example.org/tarn-survey description="Three sites, three depths, two field days. Small enough to ship." sha256=fdc42fe505d0f7f81bba9db4d4182f073040b573f02e7ecf598b0a44163feae3 >}}

The argument vocabulary is closed like every other in the tool, so a typo'd `souce=`
earns a did-you-mean instead of silently doing nothing.

## A large file, elsewhere

A file too big to ship is cited by URL, with the digest a reader needs to confirm they
got the same bytes. A remote file has nothing local to interrogate, which makes this the
case where the arguments are the only possible source of a size or a checksum:

{{< dataset https://example.org/tarn-survey/full-season.parquet title="Full-season profiles" licence=ODbL-1.0 bytes=2400000000 sha256=9f2c4a1e7b3d5860af0c19d4e2b78356cd91f0a4e6b28d7c135e9af0b64c2d81 description="Every cast of the season. Too large to ship with a book; fetch and verify." >}}

Nothing is fetched at build time. `check` stays offline, so the card is a claim the
author makes and the reader verifies, not a network call.

## What the build checks

A declared `sha256=` on an in-tree file is compared against the file on every render.
If they disagree the document reports it, which is the difference between "my figure
changed and I do not know why" and a diagnostic that says the data moved. A remote card
with no `sha256=` is reported too: without one, a reader has downloaded something and has
no way to tell whether it is the same something.
